method_specification.hyperparameters_spe.reason:
The Methodology gives concrete values for every required CETV hyperparameter:
- variogram bounds, pair cap and nugget rule;
- q floor 0.05 and IDW k ∈ {4,8,16,32};
- spectral gate: n=15, ratio 0.01, ω ∈ [2π,20π] with 113 starts, refresh every 25;
- axis-affine gate: n=40, 0.02·τ_y, 4 leaves, ζ cycle (1.00,1.15,1.15,1.15);
- local teacher: fallback to n=49, ≤8 leaves, ≥6 observations per expert, 3-fold, NNLS every 25, ζ_loc=1.25;
- polynomial degrees 1-5 with 5-fold CV and ζ_poly=1;
- candidate-bank sizes, farthest/rotating counts and the 131n offset.

The setup gives design seeds and budgets. This is sufficient to reproduce the protocol. Whether these values match the runs is an integrity question, not assessed here.

method_specification.motivation_grounded.reason:
The motivation is tied to cited active-learning and surrogate-modelling concepts, not intuition alone:
- integrated variance and information criteria (MacKay; Cohn et al.; Beck & Guillas MICE; Krause et al.);
- cross-validation and global-accuracy criteria (SFCVT, MEPE, TEAD), space-filling sequences (Crombecq) and local nonstationary emulation (Gramacy & Lee);
- an explicit argument that posterior variance misses systematic bias, while CV-residual transport assumes one failure mechanism.

The mechanism section develops the variance-versus-validated-mismatch argument further.

baseline_verification.qualitative_match_.reason:
SMART and gSMART mean IAE decreases strictly at every checkpoint on all five fields; for example, Piecewise I SMART goes 8.519→5.334→3.773→2.300. At the maximum budget both adaptive methods beat both grid and random everywhere: Piecewise I@500 is 2.300/2.063 against 2.827/3.034, and diffusion@1000 is 0.01503/0.01755 against 0.4956/0.2787. On diffusion they dominate at every budget. This is consistent with the paper's Fig. 4/Fig. 8 claims.

There are 9 early-budget exceptions out of 84 adaptive-versus-static comparisons, all disclosed:
- SMART is slightly worse than grid at Piecewise I@50, Piecewise II@50 and Piecewise II@200;
- on smooth@50 both adaptive methods are worse than grid and random;
- on smooth@100 both are worse than random.

These do not indicate misimplementation, and an exact match is not required.

